# A message-passing channel between concurrent tasks, typed by what it carries: the portable communication default
# (message passing over shared memory). Each backend wraps its platform channel (node async queue, rust tokio mpsc,
# swift buffered queue, kotlin BlockingQueue) behind this API, selected at build time, and carries the message boxed as
# `unknown`, so one shim serves every message type. send / receive are async; a buffered channel holds a value sent
# before receive is called, so send-then-receive never races.
#
# THE MESSAGE IS A TYPE. `make-channel` answers `channel t` for whatever the first `send` or the declared `like` says,
# and a message that is a form with `case` variants is received with a `fork case`, which the checker holds to every
# variant. A message the receiver has no case for is a build error, which is what Erlang's untyped `receive` cannot
# give (note/term/research/beam-otp-lessons.md, design 2). `make-text-channel` is the old text channel by name.

note stable

load @term/base/code/native/{platform}/channel
  find do-make
  find do-send
  find do-receive
  find do-close

# a channel carrying messages of type `t` (one letter, as every stdlib generic is: the native emitters spell a
# generic by upper-casing it, and a longer name reads differently where it is declared and where it is used)
form channel
  head t
  link dock, like channel-handle

  # send a message (buffered; never blocks for an unbounded channel)
  task send
    note async
    take self
    take item, like t
    like void
    call do-send
      read self/dock
      read item
      wait true

  # receive the next message, waiting if none is buffered yet
  task receive
    note async
    take self
    like t
    send back
      call do-receive
        read self/dock
        wait true

  # close the channel
  task close
    note async
    take self
    like void
    call do-close
      read self/dock
      wait true

# make a new channel. Its message type comes from how it is used
task make-channel
  head t
  like channel t
  send back
    make channel
      bind dock
        call do-make

# make a new channel of text, the old default, spelled out
task make-text-channel
  like channel text
  send back
    call make-channel
